// Life, part 5 (28 November 2026): more animals and plants.
ADDART({id:"chimpanzees",t:"Chimpanzees and Bonobos",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐒",w:"Chimpanzee",r:["gorilla","orangutans","monkeys","animalintel","humanevo","endangered","rainforests"],
p:[["Our closest relatives","Chimpanzees and bonobos share about 98–99 percent of their DNA with humans."],["Tool users","Chimpanzees use sticks, stones and leaves as tools."],["Endangered","Both species are endangered in the wild."]],
l:"Chimpanzees and bonobos are great apes that live in the forests and woodlands of central and western Africa. Together they make up the genus Pan, and they are the closest living relatives of humans: the lines leading to people and to chimpanzees split roughly six to seven million years ago, and the two species share about 98 to 99 percent of their DNA with us. Chimpanzees live across a wide belt of Africa from Senegal to Tanzania, while bonobos live only south of the Congo River in the Democratic Republic of the Congo. Both live in communities, eat mainly fruit and are highly intelligent, able to use tools, learn from one another and recognize themselves in a mirror. Chimpanzees are larger and more often aggressive, and their groups are led by males, while bonobos are slimmer, generally more peaceful and their groups are often led by females. Both species are endangered because of habitat loss, hunting for bushmeat and disease. Chimpanzees are estimated to number roughly 150,000 to 250,000 in the wild, and bonobos perhaps 30,000 to 50,000.",
s:[["Tool makers","In the 1960s the British scientist Jane Goodall watched chimpanzees at Gombe Stream in Tanzania strip leaves from twigs and poke them into termite mounds to fish out termites. Until then, many people thought only humans made tools. Chimpanzees also crack nuts with stone hammers and anvils, use leaves as sponges to drink water and sharpen sticks to hunt small animals. Different groups have different habits, which scientists describe as chimpanzee cultures."],["Life in a community","Chimpanzees live in communities of about 20 to 150 members that split into smaller parties during the day, an arrangement called fission-fusion. They groom one another to build friendships and settle tensions. Males patrol the edges of their territory, and fights between neighboring communities can be deadly. Chimpanzees also hunt, mostly monkeys, and share the meat."],["The peaceful ape?","Bonobos were only recognized as a separate species in 1929. They look like slender chimpanzees with longer legs and parted hair. Females form strong alliances, and bonobos often ease tension through play and social contact rather than fighting. Lethal attacks between groups, seen among chimpanzees, have not been observed in bonobos."],["Threats","Forests are cleared for farming, logging and mining, and roads open them to hunters. Great apes catch many human diseases, including Ebola, which has killed thousands of chimpanzees and gorillas. Protected areas, sanctuaries for orphaned apes and laws against the ape trade are part of the effort to save them."]],
k:[["Genus","Pan"],["Species","Chimpanzee and bonobo"],["Range","Central and western Africa"],["Shared DNA with humans","About 98–99 percent"],["Lifespan","About 40–50 years in the wild"],["Status","Endangered (both species)"]],
d:[["1929","Bonobo described as a separate species"],["1960","Jane Goodall begins studying chimpanzees at Gombe"],["2005","Chimpanzee genome sequenced"],["2012","Bonobo genome sequenced"]],
s2:[["Mirror test","Chimpanzees, bonobos and other great apes can pass the mirror test: when a mark is placed on their face, they use a mirror to inspect it, a sign that they recognize themselves."],["Talking with symbols","Some captive apes have learned to use hundreds of signs or symbols. A bonobo named Kanzi learned to communicate using a keyboard of picture symbols and could understand many spoken English words."]],
sp:["Jane Goodall at Gombe","Jane Goodall began observing wild chimpanzees in 1960. Her long-term study, still continuing, showed that chimpanzees make tools, hunt, have personalities and wage war, and changed how scientists think about animals and about ourselves."],
g:[["Great ape","A large, tailless primate: chimpanzees, bonobos, gorillas, orangutans and humans"],["Fission-fusion","A society that splits up and comes back together"],["Grooming","Cleaning another animal’s fur to build bonds"],["Bushmeat","Wild animals hunted for food"]],
f:["Chimpanzees use tools.","Bonobos live only in the Democratic Republic of the Congo.","Chimpanzees are the closest living relatives of humans."],
fun:"Chimpanzees build a fresh sleeping nest of bent branches in the trees almost every night."});

ADDART({id:"orangutans",t:"Orangutans",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦧",w:"Orangutan",r:["gorilla","chimpanzees","borneo","rainforests","palms","endangered","animalintel"],
p:[["People of the forest","Their name comes from Malay words meaning ‘person of the forest’."],["Tree dwellers","Orangutans are the largest animals that live mainly in trees."],["Critically endangered","All three species face a very high risk of extinction."]],
l:"Orangutans are great apes with long reddish-brown hair that live in the rainforests of Borneo and Sumatra in Southeast Asia. Their name comes from Malay words usually translated as ‘person of the forest’. They are the largest mammals that live mostly in trees, using their very long arms, which can span over two meters, and hook-like hands and feet to move through the canopy. Orangutans eat mainly fruit, especially figs and durians, along with leaves, bark, insects and honey. Unlike the other great apes, they mostly live alone, although mothers and young stay together for years. There are three species: the Bornean, the Sumatran and the Tapanuli orangutan, which was only identified as a separate species in 2017 and numbers fewer than 800. All three are critically endangered, mainly because their forests have been cleared for logging, farming and oil palm plantations and burned in fires.",
s:[["Slow childhood","A young orangutan stays with its mother for about seven to nine years, the longest childhood of any animal except humans. In that time it learns which of hundreds of plants are good to eat and where and when they fruit. Females give birth only about once every six to nine years, so populations grow very slowly and recover slowly from losses."],["Clever apes","Orangutans use sticks to pry seeds from fruit and to reach insects, and they use large leaves as umbrellas and gloves. In 2024 scientists reported a wild Sumatran orangutan called Rakus treating a wound on his face with a chewed-up medicinal plant, the first such case seen in a wild animal."],["Flanged males","Some adult males grow large cheek pads called flanges and a throat sac. They make loud ‘long calls’ that carry through the forest to attract females and warn rivals. Other males stay smaller without flanges, sometimes for years."],["Saving orangutans","Rescue centers care for orphaned orangutans and return them to the forest. Protecting large areas of forest, stopping fires and illegal hunting and producing palm oil without clearing forests are seen as the keys to their survival."]],
k:[["Genus","Pongo"],["Species","Bornean, Sumatran and Tapanuli"],["Range","Borneo and Sumatra"],["Arm span","Up to about 2.2 meters"],["Main food","Fruit"],["Status","Critically endangered (all three species)"]],
d:[["1971","Biruté Galdikas begins her long-term study in Borneo"],["2016","Bornean orangutan listed as critically endangered"],["2017","Tapanuli orangutan described as a new species"],["2024","An orangutan is seen treating a wound with a medicinal plant"]],
s2:[["A loner among apes","Orangutans spread out because fruit in the rainforest is patchy. A group would quickly strip a fruit tree, so each adult forages over its own area."],["Nest builders","Like other great apes, orangutans build a new nest of branches and leaves each night, sometimes adding a roof against the rain."]],
sp:["The newest great ape","In 2017 scientists announced that a small population of orangutans in the Batang Toru forest of North Sumatra was a separate species, the Tapanuli orangutan, the first new great ape species named in nearly 90 years."],
g:[["Canopy","The upper layer of a forest"],["Flange","A cheek pad grown by some adult male orangutans"],["Long call","A loud call made by male orangutans"],["Oil palm","A palm grown for palm oil"]],
f:["Orangutans mostly live alone.","Orangutans live only in Borneo and Sumatra.","A young orangutan stays with its mother for years."],
fun:"An orangutan’s arms are much longer than its legs; standing upright, its fingers almost reach its ankles."});

ADDART({id:"anteaters",t:"Anteaters",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐜",w:"Anteater",r:["sloths","armadillos","pangolins","ants","termite","grasslands","rainforests"],
p:[["No teeth","Anteaters have no teeth at all."],["Super tongue","The giant anteater’s tongue can be about 60 centimeters long."],["Picky eaters","They feed almost entirely on ants and termites."]],
l:"Anteaters are mammals of Central and South America that feed almost entirely on ants and termites. They have long, tube-shaped snouts, no teeth and extremely long, sticky tongues covered in tiny backward-pointing spines. There are four kinds, in two groups of species: the giant anteater, which can be over two meters long including its bushy tail; the two tamanduas, which are about half that size and climb trees; and the tiny silky anteater, small enough to fit in a hand. Anteaters use their powerful front claws to rip open ant and termite nests, then flick their tongues in and out up to about 150 times a minute. They feed at each nest for only a minute or so before moving on, which avoids painful stings and leaves the colony to recover. Anteaters are related to sloths and armadillos. The giant anteater is listed as vulnerable, threatened by habitat loss, fires, road traffic and hunting.",
s:[["Built for insects","An anteater’s stomach grinds its food with the help of swallowed sand and small stones, since it cannot chew. A giant anteater may eat around 30,000 ants and termites a day. Its sense of smell is about 40 times better than a human’s, which it uses to find nests."],["Dangerous claws","The giant anteater walks on its knuckles to keep its long front claws sharp. When threatened, it rears up on its hind legs and slashes with its claws, and it can fight off a jaguar or puma."],["Babies on board","A baby giant anteater rides on its mother’s back for months. The stripe on its fur lines up with the stripe on hers, which may help hide it from predators."],["Not the same as aardvarks or pangolins","Aardvarks of Africa and pangolins of Africa and Asia also eat ants and termites with long tongues, but they are not closely related to anteaters. Their similar shapes are an example of convergent evolution, when different animals evolve similar features for similar ways of life."]],
k:[["Range","Central and South America"],["Kinds","Giant anteater, two tamanduas, silky anteaters"],["Teeth","None"],["Giant anteater tongue","About 60 centimeters long"],["Relatives","Sloths and armadillos"],["Giant anteater status","Vulnerable"]],
s2:[["Low body temperature","Giant anteaters have one of the lowest body temperatures of any land mammal, about 33 degrees Celsius, because their insect diet gives them little energy."],["Tree climbers","Tamanduas and silky anteaters use gripping tails to climb trees. The silky anteater spends almost its whole life in the treetops."]],
g:[["Tamandua","A medium-sized, tree-climbing anteater"],["Convergent evolution","When unrelated animals evolve similar features"],["Xenarthra","The group that includes anteaters, sloths and armadillos"],["Vulnerable","At risk of becoming endangered"]],
f:["Anteaters have no teeth.","Anteaters are related to sloths.","Giant anteaters walk on their knuckles."],
fun:"A giant anteater can flick its tongue in and out of an ant nest about two to three times a second."});

ADDART({id:"antelopes",t:"Antelopes and Gazelles",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦌",w:"Antelope",r:["grasslands","animalmig","zebras","cheetah","lion","giraffe","deer"],
p:[["Horned grazers","Antelopes are hoofed animals with hollow horns that are never shed."],["Built for speed","Many can sprint at more than 80 kilometers per hour."],["Great migrations","Wildebeest make one of the largest migrations on Earth."]],
l:"Antelopes are a varied group of hoofed, plant-eating mammals that belong to the same family as cattle, sheep and goats. Most live in Africa, with others in Asia, and they range from the dik-dik, about 30 to 40 centimeters tall at the shoulder, to the giant eland, which can weigh nearly a ton. Antelopes have hollow horns made of a bony core covered in keratin, the material of fingernails, and unlike the antlers of deer, the horns are never shed. Many species, such as gazelles, impalas and springboks, live in herds on open grasslands and rely on speed, sharp senses and safety in numbers to escape predators such as lions, cheetahs and wild dogs. Others, such as the bongo and duikers, live hidden in forests. The pronghorn of North America looks like an antelope but belongs to its own family. Some antelopes are common, while others, such as the addax and the saiga, are critically endangered.",
s:[["The great migration","Each year more than a million wildebeest, along with zebras and gazelles, move in a great loop through the Serengeti of Tanzania and the Maasai Mara of Kenya, following the rains and fresh grass. Crossing rivers full of crocodiles is the most dangerous part of the journey. About half a million calves are born within a few weeks early in the year, so predators cannot eat them all."],["Pronking","Springboks and some gazelles make high, stiff-legged leaps called pronking or stotting. Scientists think it signals to a predator that the antelope is fit and not worth chasing."],["Desert survivors","The oryx and the addax can live in deserts for long periods without drinking, getting water from the plants they eat and letting their body temperature rise in the heat of the day to save water."],["Comebacks and crashes","The Arabian oryx became extinct in the wild in 1972 and was brought back by breeding animals in captivity and releasing them. The saiga of Central Asia lost more than 200,000 animals to a bacterial disease in 2015, but its numbers have since recovered strongly in Kazakhstan."]],
k:[["Family","Bovidae (with cattle, sheep and goats)"],["Number of species","About 90"],["Smallest","Royal antelope and dik-diks"],["Largest","Giant eland"],["Horns","Hollow and never shed"],["Main range","Africa and Asia"]],
d:[["1972","Arabian oryx goes extinct in the wild"],["1982","Arabian oryx reintroduced in Oman"],["2011","Arabian oryx is the first species to return to vulnerable after being extinct in the wild"],["2015","Mass die-off of saiga antelopes"]],
s2:[["Horns versus antlers","Antlers, found on deer, are bone and fall off and regrow every year. Horns, found on antelopes, have a bone core inside a keratin sheath and keep growing throughout life."],["Tiny dik-dik","Dik-diks live in pairs and mark their territory with scent from glands in front of their eyes. Their name comes from their alarm call."]],
g:[["Bovid","A member of the cattle family"],["Keratin","A tough protein found in horns, hair and nails"],["Pronking","High leaps made by some antelopes"],["Ruminant","An animal that chews the cud and has a stomach with several chambers"]],
f:["Antelope horns are never shed.","Wildebeest are antelopes.","The pronghorn is not a true antelope."],
fun:"A Thomson’s gazelle can change direction in mid-sprint so sharply that a cheetah sometimes overshoots it."});

ADDART({id:"sheepgoats",t:"Sheep and Goats",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐑",w:"Sheep",r:["domestication","cattle","agri","cloning","textiles","grasslands","antelopes"],
p:[["Among the first farm animals","Sheep and goats were domesticated more than 10,000 years ago."],["Wool, milk and meat","They give people wool, milk, cheese, meat and leather."],["Hardy climbers","Wild goats and sheep can live on steep mountain slopes."]],
l:"Sheep and goats are hoofed, plant-eating mammals in the same family as cattle and antelopes, and they were among the first animals ever domesticated. Goats were tamed from the wild bezoar goat and sheep from the wild mouflon in the Middle East about 10,000 to 11,000 years ago. Today there are well over a billion of each kind around the world, raised for meat, milk, wool, hair and leather. Sheep mostly graze on grass, while goats are browsers that nibble leaves, shrubs and twigs and can thrive where sheep would struggle. Domestic sheep usually have a thick fleece that must be sheared, and goats give mohair and cashmere as well as milk used for cheese. Wild relatives, such as the ibex, the bighorn sheep and the markhor, are expert climbers on cliffs and mountains. In 1996 a sheep named Dolly became the first mammal cloned from an adult cell.",
s:[["Telling them apart","Goats usually have tails that point up and many have beards, while sheep have tails that hang down. Sheep tend to flock tightly and follow a leader, while goats are more independent and curious. Goats have rectangular pupils, as do sheep, which give them a wide field of view to watch for predators."],["Wool","Wild sheep have a coarse coat with a short woolly undercoat that they shed each year. Over thousands of years people bred sheep with fine wool that keeps growing, so most domestic sheep now need to be sheared. Merino sheep produce some of the finest wool, used for clothing."],["Mountain experts","Ibex and markhor climb near-vertical slopes and even the steep walls of dams in search of salt. Their hooves have hard edges with soft, grippy pads in the middle that help them grip rock."],["Dolly the sheep","Dolly was born on 5 July 1996 at the Roslin Institute in Scotland. She was cloned from a cell taken from an adult sheep’s udder, proving that an adult cell could be used to make a whole new animal. She lived until 2003."]],
k:[["Family","Bovidae"],["Domesticated","About 10,000–11,000 years ago"],["Wild ancestors","Mouflon (sheep), bezoar goat (goats)"],["Products","Wool, milk, cheese, meat, leather"],["Famous sheep","Dolly, the first cloned mammal from an adult cell"]],
d:[["c. 9000 BCE","Sheep and goats domesticated in the Middle East"],["1797","Merino sheep arrive in Australia"],["1996","Dolly the sheep is born"],["2003","Dolly dies"]],
s2:[["Goat’s milk","Goat’s milk is drunk and made into cheeses such as feta, which is often a mix of sheep and goat milk."],["Cashmere","Cashmere is the soft undercoat combed from certain goats, named after the Kashmir region."]],
g:[["Fleece","The coat of wool on a sheep"],["Shearing","Cutting off a sheep’s wool"],["Browser","An animal that eats leaves, twigs and shrubs"],["Clone","A genetically identical copy of a living thing"]],
f:["Goats are browsers rather than grazers.","Dolly the sheep was cloned from an adult cell.","Sheep and goats were domesticated in the Middle East."],
fun:"Goats were so useful on long sea voyages that sailors once left them on islands as a future food supply."});

ADDART({id:"pigs",t:"Pigs and Wild Boars",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐖",w:"Pig",r:["domestication","cattle","animalintel","invasiveplants","agri","hippo","sheepgoats"],
p:[["Clever animals","Pigs are among the most intelligent domestic animals."],["Wild ancestors","Domestic pigs come from the wild boar."],["Mud for cooling","Pigs roll in mud because they can barely sweat."]],
l:"Pigs are stout, hoofed mammals with a flat snout that they use to dig for food. The domestic pig is descended from the wild boar, which once lived across Europe, Asia and North Africa, and it was domesticated separately in the Middle East and in China about 9,000 to 10,000 years ago. Today there are roughly a billion domestic pigs in the world, kept mainly for meat. Pigs eat almost anything, from roots, fruit and nuts to insects and small animals. They have an excellent sense of smell and are among the most intelligent farm animals, able to learn tricks, use mirrors to find hidden food and recognize other pigs and people. Wild relatives include warthogs of Africa, the babirusa of Indonesia, with tusks that grow through its snout, and the tiny pygmy hog of the Himalayan foothills. Wild boars and feral pigs that have escaped from farms are an invasive pest in many places, including the Americas and Australia.",
s:[["Why mud?","Pigs have very few working sweat glands, so they keep cool by wallowing in mud or water. A coat of mud also protects their skin from sunburn and insects. Contrary to their reputation, pigs avoid soiling the places where they sleep and eat when given enough space."],["Truffle hunters","Pigs’ powerful noses can find truffles, valuable fungi that grow underground. Female pigs were traditionally used in France and Italy, but they tend to eat the truffles, so many hunters now use trained dogs instead."],["Smart and social","Wild boars live in groups called sounders, made up of females and their young. Pigs communicate with grunts, squeals and barks, and piglets learn their mother’s voice within days. In tests, pigs can learn to move a cursor on a screen with a joystick."],["Pigs and people","Pigs have provided meat, leather and bristles for brushes, and their heart valves are used to repair human hearts. In 2022 doctors in the United States transplanted a genetically modified pig heart into a person for the first time, and pig kidneys have since been transplanted too."]],
k:[["Wild ancestor","Wild boar"],["Domesticated","About 9,000–10,000 years ago"],["Domestic pigs in the world","Roughly a billion"],["Diet","Omnivorous"],["Relatives","Warthogs, babirusas, pygmy hogs"]],
d:[["c. 8500 BCE","Pigs domesticated in the Middle East"],["1493","Pigs arrive in the Americas with Columbus"],["2022","First pig heart transplanted into a human"]],
s2:[["Feral pigs","Feral pigs dig up crops and wild plants and spread disease. In the United States they cause an estimated billions of dollars in damage a year."],["Pigs in culture","The pig is one of the 12 animals of the Chinese zodiac, and pigs appear in many children’s stories and films."]],
g:[["Feral","An animal that has escaped from domestication and lives wild"],["Sounder","A group of wild pigs"],["Wallowing","Rolling in mud or water"],["Omnivore","An animal that eats plants and animals"]],
f:["Pigs can barely sweat.","Domestic pigs come from wild boars.","Pigs were domesticated more than once."],
fun:"A wild boar’s strong snout can plow up the ground like a small bulldozer as it searches for roots and grubs."});

ADDART({id:"falcons",t:"Hawks and Falcons",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦅",w:"Falcon",r:["eagle","owl","vultures","birds","birdmigr","extinction","conservation"],
p:[["Raptors","Hawks and falcons are birds of prey with hooked beaks and sharp talons."],["Fastest animal","A diving peregrine falcon can top 300 kilometers per hour."],["A comeback story","Falcons recovered after the pesticide DDT was banned."]],
l:"Hawks and falcons are birds of prey, or raptors, that hunt other animals using keen eyesight, hooked beaks and sharp, curved claws called talons. Although they look similar, the two groups are not closely related. Hawks belong to the same family as eagles and kites, while genetic studies show that falcons are more closely related to parrots and songbirds. Hawks usually kill with their talons and have rounded wings for twisting through trees, while falcons have long, pointed wings for speed and often kill with a bite from a notched beak. The peregrine falcon, found on every continent except Antarctica, is the fastest animal on Earth, reaching speeds of over 300 kilometers per hour as it dives on prey. In the mid-1900s peregrines and other raptors declined sharply because the pesticide DDT made their eggshells thin. After DDT was banned in many countries, peregrines recovered, and many now nest on skyscrapers and bridges in cities.",
s:[["Eyes like telescopes","A raptor’s eyes are huge for its head and packed with light-sensing cells. A hawk can spot a mouse from high in the sky. Many raptors also see ultraviolet light, which may help them follow the urine trails of voles."],["The stoop","A hunting peregrine climbs high, folds its wings and drops in a steep dive called a stoop. Special bones in its nostrils are thought to slow the air rushing in, so it can breathe at high speed, and a third eyelid protects its eyes."],["Falconry","People have trained hawks and falcons to hunt for at least 4,000 years in Asia and the Middle East. Falconry was a sport of kings in medieval Europe and remains popular in the Gulf states. UNESCO lists it as part of humanity’s intangible cultural heritage."],["The DDT story","DDT built up in the food chain and caused birds of prey to lay eggs with shells so thin they broke during incubation. Rachel Carson’s 1962 book Silent Spring helped raise the alarm. After bans in the 1970s and captive breeding programs, the peregrine was removed from the US endangered species list in 1999."]],
k:[["Group","Birds of prey (raptors)"],["Hawks’ family","Accipitridae (with eagles)"],["Falcons’ family","Falconidae"],["Fastest dive","Peregrine falcon, over 300 km/h"],["Smallest falcon","Falconets, about 15 centimeters long"]],
d:[["1962","Silent Spring warns about DDT"],["1972","DDT banned for most uses in the United States"],["1999","Peregrine falcon removed from the US endangered list"],["2010","Falconry added to UNESCO’s heritage list"]],
s2:[["City birds","Peregrines nest on tall buildings, where pigeons provide plenty of food. Many city nests have webcams that thousands of people watch."],["Kestrels","Kestrels are small falcons that hover in place, facing into the wind, while they look for mice and insects on the ground."]],
g:[["Raptor","A bird of prey"],["Talon","A sharp, curved claw"],["Stoop","A falcon’s high-speed dive"],["Pesticide","A chemical used to kill pests"]],
f:["Falcons are more closely related to parrots than to hawks.","The peregrine is the fastest animal.","DDT made raptor eggshells thin."],
fun:"Some falcons in the Middle East have their own passports so they can travel with their owners."});

ADDART({id:"herons",t:"Herons and Cranes",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪶",w:"Heron",r:["birds","storks","flamingo","wetlands","birdmigr","pelicans","fishes"],
p:[["Long-legged waders","Herons and cranes are tall birds with long legs and necks."],["Patient hunters","Herons stand still for long periods waiting to spear fish."],["Dancing birds","Cranes perform leaping courtship dances."]],
l:"Herons and cranes are tall, long-legged birds that live in wetlands, rivers and grasslands around the world. They look alike, but they belong to different groups. Herons, which include egrets and bitterns, are patient hunters that wade in shallow water or stand still at the water’s edge, then strike with a sharp, dagger-like bill to catch fish, frogs and other small animals. In flight they fold their necks into an S shape. Cranes are larger, fly with their necks stretched straight out and eat a wider mix of plants, seeds, insects and small animals. Cranes are famous for their loud, bugling calls and their dances, in which pairs bow, leap and flap their wings. Several crane species are endangered because wetlands have been drained. The whooping crane of North America fell to about 15 to 20 birds in the 1940s but has slowly recovered with protection and captive breeding.",
s:[["Clever fishing","Green herons and striated herons drop bits of bread, insects or feathers onto the water as bait to lure fish within reach, one of the few known examples of tool use by birds. Black herons spread their wings like an umbrella over the water, making shade that attracts fish and helps them see."],["Dances and lifelong pairs","Cranes usually pair for life. Their dances strengthen pair bonds and are also seen in young birds and flocks. Many cultures have crane dances, and in East Asia the red-crowned crane is a symbol of long life and good fortune."],["Long journeys","Many cranes migrate thousands of kilometers. Demoiselle cranes cross the Himalayas, flying at high altitude, between Central Asia and India, and huge flocks of common cranes stop at traditional resting places in Europe each year."],["Plumes and protection","In the late 1800s millions of egrets were killed for their breeding feathers, which decorated women’s hats. Outrage over the trade helped start bird protection groups such as the Audubon societies and early laws protecting wild birds."]],
k:[["Herons","Family Ardeidae, including egrets and bitterns"],["Cranes","Family Gruidae"],["Neck in flight","Herons fold it; cranes stretch it out"],["Tallest flying bird","Sarus crane, up to about 1.8 meters"],["Endangered example","Whooping crane"]],
d:[["1886","First Audubon Society founded, partly to stop the plume trade"],["1918","US Migratory Bird Treaty Act protects egrets"],["1941","Only about 15–20 wild whooping cranes remain"]],
s2:[["A thousand cranes","In Japan, folding a thousand paper cranes is said to grant a wish. After the atomic bombing of Hiroshima, the story of Sadako Sasaki made paper cranes a symbol of peace."],["Bitterns","Bitterns are secretive herons. When alarmed they point their bills upward and sway like reeds to stay hidden."]],
g:[["Wading bird","A bird that feeds in shallow water"],["Egret","A kind of heron, often white"],["Courtship","Behavior that attracts a mate"],["Plume","A long, showy feather"]],
f:["Herons fold their necks in flight.","Some herons use bait to catch fish.","Cranes often pair for life."],
fun:"A great blue heron can swallow a fish wider than its own neck, which stretches to let it pass."});

ADDART({id:"clownfish",t:"Clownfish and Sea Anemones",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐠",w:"Amphiprioninae",r:["coral","fishes","symbiosis","jelly","climchange","seahorse","octopus"],
p:[["Best partners","Clownfish live safely among the stinging tentacles of sea anemones."],["Change of sex","A clownfish can turn from male to female."],["Reef residents","They live on coral reefs in the Indian and Pacific oceans."]],
l:"Clownfish, also called anemonefish, are small, brightly colored fish that live on coral reefs in the warm waters of the Indian and Pacific oceans. There are about 30 species, most of them orange, red, yellow or black with white bands. Clownfish are famous for living among the tentacles of sea anemones, animals related to jellyfish and corals whose tentacles carry stinging cells that kill or stun other small fish. A layer of mucus on the clownfish’s skin protects it from the stings. The partnership helps both: the anemone shelters the clownfish and its eggs from predators, while the clownfish chases away fish that eat anemones, cleans the anemone and provides nutrients from its waste. Each anemone usually holds one group, ruled by the largest fish, a female. If she dies, the largest male changes into a female and takes her place. Like coral reefs, anemones can bleach in hot water, putting clownfish at risk.",
s:[["Sea anemones","Sea anemones are soft-bodied animals that attach themselves to rocks or coral with a sticky foot. Their tentacles fire tiny harpoon-like stinging cells called nematocysts. Of more than 1,000 species of anemone, only about ten host clownfish."],["How the protection works","Scientists still debate exactly how clownfish avoid stings. Their mucus is thicker than that of other fish and seems to keep the anemone’s stinging cells from firing. Young clownfish may build up protection by touching the anemone gently at first."],["Family life","Male clownfish guard and fan the eggs, which are laid on a rock beside the anemone. Hatchlings drift in the open ocean for a week or two, then settle on the reef, finding a home anemone partly by smell and sound."],["Popularity and threats","Clownfish became hugely popular after appearing in animated films, and many are now bred in captivity for aquariums. Wild clownfish face threats from warming oceans, which can cause anemones to bleach, and from collecting for the aquarium trade."]],
k:[["Number of species","About 30"],["Habitat","Coral reefs of the Indian and Pacific oceans"],["Partner","Sea anemones"],["Size","Up to about 11–17 centimeters"],["Family leader","The largest female"]],
s2:[["Mutualism","The clownfish and anemone partnership is a classic example of mutualism, a relationship in which both partners benefit."],["Clownfish stripes","Scientists found that young clownfish gain their white stripes one at a time, and how quickly they gain them can depend on the species of anemone they live in."]],
g:[["Anemone","A soft-bodied sea animal with stinging tentacles"],["Nematocyst","A stinging cell"],["Mutualism","A partnership that helps both sides"],["Bleaching","When corals or anemones lose the algae that live in them and turn pale"]],
f:["Clownfish are protected by mucus.","Clownfish can change from male to female.","Only a few kinds of anemone host clownfish."],
fun:"Clownfish make popping and clicking sounds to defend their anemone and to keep order within their group."});

ADDART({id:"sponges",t:"Sea Sponges",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🧽",w:"Sponge",r:["coral","echinoderms","deepsea","evo","cell","jelly","originoflife"],
p:[["Simplest animals","Sponges have no brain, muscles, nerves or organs."],["Living filters","They pump water through their bodies to catch food."],["Ancient","Sponges may be one of the oldest groups of animals."]],
l:"Sea sponges are simple animals that live attached to rocks, coral and the seabed, mostly in the ocean, with some in fresh water. They have no brain, nerves, muscles or true organs. Instead, a sponge’s body is a mass of cells around a network of pores and channels. Special cells with whip-like hairs beat to pull water in through tiny pores, trap bacteria and food particles, and push the water out through larger openings. A sponge the size of a person’s hand can filter many liters of water an hour. Sponges come in every shape, from crusts and cups to tubes, barrels and branching fans, and range from a few millimeters to more than three meters across. There are around 9,000 known species. Their bodies are supported by tiny mineral spikes called spicules or by a soft protein called spongin. Sponges are one of the earliest groups of animals, and some scientists think they may be the very first branch on the animal family tree.",
s:[["How a sponge works","Sponge cells are surprisingly independent. If some sponges are pushed through a fine sieve so that their cells separate, the cells can crawl back together and rebuild a working sponge. This makes sponges useful for studying how animal bodies first evolved."],["Reproduction","Sponges can reproduce by breaking off pieces that grow into new sponges. Most also release sperm into the water, and some release clouds that look like smoke. The larvae swim briefly before settling and growing into a new sponge."],["Glass sponges and long lives","Glass sponges in the deep sea build skeletons of silica, the material of glass. Some deep-sea sponges are thought to be thousands of years old, making them among the longest-lived animals on Earth."],["Sponges and people","For thousands of years people harvested natural sponges for washing, especially in the Mediterranean. Most household sponges today are synthetic. Chemicals made by sponges to defend themselves have led to medicines, including drugs used against some cancers and viruses."]],
k:[["Known species","About 9,000"],["Phylum","Porifera, meaning ‘pore bearer’"],["Organs","None"],["Feeding","Filtering water"],["Skeleton","Mineral spicules or spongin"],["Oldest fossils","Probably over 500 million years old"]],
s2:[["Not a plant","Sponges were once thought to be plants because they do not move. In the 1700s and 1800s scientists showed that they are animals, since they eat other living things."],["Venus’s flower basket","This glass sponge often houses a pair of shrimp that enter when young and grow too big to leave, so in Japan it has been a traditional wedding gift and symbol of lasting love."]],
g:[["Porifera","The animal group of sponges"],["Spicule","A tiny mineral spike that supports a sponge"],["Filter feeder","An animal that strains food from water"],["Larva","The young form of an animal that changes as it grows"]],
f:["Sponges are animals.","Sponges have no brain or nerves.","Some sponges live thousands of years."],
fun:"Some carnivorous sponges in the deep sea trap tiny shrimp on hook-covered threads instead of filtering water."});

ADDART({id:"krill",t:"Krill",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦐",w:"Krill",r:["antarc","whale","penguin","plankton","foodchains","crustacean","seals"],
p:[["Tiny but vital","Krill are small shrimp-like animals that feed much of the Southern Ocean."],["Huge swarms","Their swarms can be seen from space."],["Glowing bodies","Krill can make their own light."]],
l:"Krill are small, shrimp-like crustaceans that live in oceans around the world. There are about 85 species, but the best known is Antarctic krill, which grows to about six centimeters long and lives in the Southern Ocean around Antarctica. Antarctic krill are one of the most abundant animals on Earth: their total weight is estimated at hundreds of millions of tonnes. They gather in swarms so dense that they can turn the water pink and can be seen from space. Krill feed on tiny drifting algae, especially those that grow under sea ice, and in turn they are eaten by whales, seals, penguins, seabirds, fish and squid, making them the center of the Antarctic food web. A blue whale can eat several tonnes of krill a day. People also catch krill for fish food, animal feed and health supplements. The catch is managed by an international body, the Commission for the Conservation of Antarctic Marine Living Resources (CCAMLR), and in 2025 and 2026 the fishery reached its catch limit of 620,000 tonnes early, closing the season.",
s:[["Life under the ice","In winter, young krill shelter and feed under the sea ice, grazing algae that grow on its underside. As the climate warms and sea ice shrinks in parts of Antarctica, scientists worry that krill numbers could fall, with effects all the way up the food chain."],["Glowing and shrinking","Krill have light organs that glow blue-green, which may help hide them from predators below by matching faint light from above or help them communicate in swarms. When food is scarce, krill can survive for months and even shrink in size."],["Carbon pump","Krill eat algae near the surface and release droppings that sink quickly to the deep ocean. This moves large amounts of carbon out of the surface water, which helps slow the build-up of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere."],["Managing the catch","Most of the krill catch is taken near the Antarctic Peninsula and the South Orkney Islands. Conservation groups worry that fishing concentrated near penguin and whale feeding areas could harm them, while the fishing industry has asked for a higher catch limit. CCAMLR sets the rules by agreement among its members."]],
k:[["Species","About 85"],["Antarctic krill length","Up to about 6 centimeters"],["Main food","Algae and plankton"],["Eaten by","Whales, seals, penguins, seabirds, fish"],["Catch limit (Antarctic)","620,000 tonnes a year (trigger level)"],["Managed by","CCAMLR"]],
d:[["1982","CCAMLR convention comes into force"],["2025","Krill fishery closes early after reaching its limit"],["2026","Fishery reaches its limit early again"]],
s2:[["Whale food","The word krill comes from Norwegian, meaning young fish. Whalers noticed that the great whales of the Southern Ocean fed on vast swarms of the tiny animals."],["Molting","Like other crustaceans, krill shed their hard outer skeletons to grow. A krill can also molt suddenly to leave a predator holding an empty shell."]],
g:[["Crustacean","An animal with a hard outer skeleton and jointed legs, such as a crab or shrimp"],["Swarm","A large, dense group of animals"],["Bioluminescence","Light made by a living thing"],["Food web","The network of who eats whom"]],
f:["Krill are crustaceans.","Krill can make their own light.","Antarctic krill feed on algae under sea ice."],
fun:"A single swarm of Antarctic krill can contain millions of tonnes of animals packed into the water."});

ADDART({id:"centipedes",t:"Centipedes and Millipedes",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐛",w:"Myriapoda",r:["insect","spider","scorpions","worms","decomposers","soil","venom"],
p:[["Many legs","Centipedes and millipedes are myriapods, animals with many legs."],["Hunter and recycler","Centipedes hunt; millipedes mostly eat dead plants."],["Not really a thousand","Only one known millipede has more than 1,000 legs."]],
l:"Centipedes and millipedes are myriapods, a group of land animals with long bodies made of many segments, each bearing legs. Although they look alike, they live very different lives. Centipedes are fast, flattened predators with one pair of legs on each segment. Their front legs have become hollow fangs that inject venom, which they use to kill insects, spiders, worms and, in the largest species, even small mice, lizards and bats. Millipedes are slower and rounder, with two pairs of legs on most segments. They feed mainly on rotting leaves and wood, helping to break down dead plants and recycle nutrients into the soil. Instead of biting, many millipedes defend themselves by curling into a coil and releasing bad-tasting or toxic chemicals. Despite their names, centipedes have from about 15 to 191 pairs of legs, never exactly 100, and most millipedes have fewer than 200 legs. In 2021 scientists described an Australian millipede with up to 1,306 legs, the first known to have more than a thousand.",
s:[["Counting legs","Centipedes always have an odd number of leg pairs, so none can have exactly 50 pairs, or 100 legs. A newly hatched millipede may have only three pairs of legs and adds more segments each time it molts."],["Giant centipedes","The Amazonian giant centipede can grow to about 30 centimeters long. In caves in Venezuela it has been seen hanging from the ceiling to catch bats in flight."],["Chemical defenses","Some millipedes release hydrogen cyanide or other chemicals. In parts of the tropics, monkeys and birds rub millipedes on their fur or feathers, and scientists think the chemicals may repel mosquitoes."],["Early land animals","Myriapods were among the first animals to live on land, more than 400 million years ago. Arthropleura, a millipede relative from about 300 million years ago, grew longer than two meters and is the largest land invertebrate known."]],
k:[["Group","Myriapods"],["Centipede legs","One pair per segment, 15 to 191 pairs"],["Millipede legs","Two pairs on most segments"],["Most legs","1,306 (Eumillipes persephone)"],["Centipede diet","Animals"],["Millipede diet","Mostly dead plants"]],
d:[["c. 420 million years ago","Early myriapods live on land"],["c. 300 million years ago","Giant Arthropleura roams coal forests"],["2021","First millipede with over 1,000 legs described"]],
s2:[["House centipedes","House centipedes, with 15 pairs of very long legs, often live indoors and hunt cockroaches, silverfish and other pests."],["Pill millipedes","Pill millipedes roll into a ball when disturbed, much like pill bugs, which are actually crustaceans."]],
g:[["Myriapod","A many-legged arthropod such as a centipede or millipede"],["Segment","One of the repeated sections of a body"],["Forcipules","A centipede’s venom fangs"],["Invertebrate","An animal without a backbone"]],
f:["No centipede has exactly 100 legs.","Millipedes help recycle dead plants.","Centipedes have venom."],
fun:"The millipede with 1,306 legs lives up to 60 meters underground and has no eyes."});

ADDART({id:"grasshoppers",t:"Grasshoppers and Crickets",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦗",w:"Grasshopper",r:["insect","locusts","grasslands","animalcomm","metamorph","mantises","cicadas"],
p:[["Powerful jumpers","Grasshoppers can leap about 20 times their body length."],["Insect musicians","Crickets and grasshoppers make sounds to attract mates."],["Locust swarms","Some grasshoppers can turn into swarming locusts."]],
l:"Grasshoppers and crickets are insects with strong back legs built for jumping. Together with katydids, they belong to the order Orthoptera, meaning ‘straight wings’, which has more than 25,000 species. Grasshoppers have short antennae, are active by day and eat plants, especially grasses. Crickets and katydids have long, thread-like antennae, are often active at night and eat a wider mix of plants and small animals. Many of them make sounds to attract mates. Crickets and katydids chirp by rubbing one wing against the other, while grasshoppers usually rub a back leg against a wing. Their ‘ears’ are in unusual places: on the front legs in crickets and katydids, and on the sides of the abdomen in grasshoppers. Young grasshoppers hatch looking like small wingless adults, called nymphs, and grow by molting. Under crowded conditions some grasshoppers change color and behavior and form huge, destructive swarms known as locusts.",
s:[["Jumping","A grasshopper’s back legs work like a catapult. It slowly bends them, storing energy in springy parts of its knees, then releases it all at once. Some can leap about 20 times their body length."],["Chirping thermometer","Crickets chirp faster when it is warm and slower when it is cool, because their body temperature follows the air. In 1897 the American physicist Amos Dolbear worked out a formula for estimating the temperature from the chirp rate of the snowy tree cricket."],["Disguise","Many katydids look exactly like leaves, complete with veins, holes and patches of mold. Some grasshoppers are colored like stones or sand, and some flash bright hind wings when they jump to startle predators."],["Food and pets","Grasshoppers and crickets are eaten in many countries, such as chapulines in Mexico, and are farmed as a protein-rich food for people and for pets. In China, crickets have been kept as singing pets in small cages for centuries, and cricket fighting is a traditional sport."]],
k:[["Order","Orthoptera"],["Number of species","More than 25,000"],["Grasshoppers","Short antennae, active by day"],["Crickets and katydids","Long antennae, often active at night"],["Young","Nymphs, which molt to grow"]],
d:[["1897","Dolbear’s law links cricket chirps and temperature"]],
s2:[["Cave crickets","Cave crickets have no wings and very long legs and antennae for finding their way in the dark."],["Mole crickets","Mole crickets have shovel-like front legs for digging and sing from burrows shaped to amplify the sound."]],
g:[["Orthoptera","The insect order of grasshoppers and crickets"],["Nymph","A young insect that looks like a small adult"],["Stridulation","Making sound by rubbing body parts together"],["Antennae","Feelers on an insect’s head"]],
f:["Crickets hear with their front legs.","Locusts are a kind of grasshopper.","Crickets chirp faster when it is warmer."],
fun:"Counting a snowy tree cricket’s chirps in 14 seconds and adding 40 gives a rough temperature in degrees Fahrenheit."});

ADDART({id:"ginkgo",t:"The Ginkgo Tree",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🌳",w:"Ginkgo biloba",r:["trees","conifers","fossils","medicinalplants","atomicbomb","seeds","dinosaurs"],
p:[["A living fossil","The ginkgo has changed little in about 200 million years."],["Fan-shaped leaves","Its leaves turn bright gold in autumn."],["Tough survivor","Ginkgos survived the atomic bombing of Hiroshima."]],
l:"The ginkgo, or maidenhair tree, is a tall tree with fan-shaped leaves that is often called a living fossil. It is the only living member of a group of plants that was widespread in the time of the dinosaurs, and fossil leaves almost identical to today’s are found in rocks about 200 million years old. By about two million years ago ginkgos had disappeared from most of the world, surviving only in a few places in China, where they were cared for in temple gardens. From there people spread them to Korea and Japan and, in the 1700s, to Europe and North America. Today ginkgos are planted along streets in many cities because they tolerate pollution, pests, drought and poor soil. Ginkgos are either male or female. Female trees produce seeds with a fleshy coat that smells unpleasant when it falls and rots, so male trees are usually chosen for streets. The seed kernels are eaten in East Asian cooking, and extracts of the leaves are sold as supplements.",
s:[["Neither conifer nor flowering plant","Ginkgos are gymnosperms, plants with seeds that are not enclosed in fruit, like conifers. But they belong to their own division, Ginkgophyta. Their sperm cells swim using tiny hairs, a feature shared only with cycads among seed plants and a reminder of their ancient origins."],["Hiroshima survivors","Several ginkgo trees about one to two kilometers from the center of the atomic explosion at Hiroshima in 1945 were badly burned but sprouted again the next spring. They still grow there today and are known in Japan as hibakujumoku, survivor trees, and symbols of hope."],["Golden autumn","In autumn ginkgo leaves turn a brilliant yellow, and many trees drop almost all their leaves within a few days. Ginkgo-lined streets, such as those in Tokyo, Seoul and New York, draw crowds to see them."],["Old trees","Some ginkgos in China, Korea and Japan are thought to be more than 1,000 years old. Studies of old ginkgos found that their cells show few signs of aging, which may help explain their long lives."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Ginkgo biloba"],["Other name","Maidenhair tree"],["Native range","China"],["Oldest fossils of similar leaves","About 200 million years old"],["Height","Up to about 30–40 meters"],["Trees","Separate male and female trees"]],
d:[["c. 200 million years ago","Ginkgo-like trees grow worldwide"],["1712","Engelbert Kaempfer describes the ginkgo for Europeans"],["1945","Ginkgos survive the Hiroshima bombing"]],
s2:[["The name","The name ginkgo comes from a misspelling of a Japanese reading of the Chinese name, meaning ‘silver apricot’."],["Two-lobed leaves","The species name biloba means ‘two-lobed’, because many leaves have a notch dividing them into two halves."]],
g:[["Living fossil","A living species very similar to ancient fossil forms"],["Gymnosperm","A plant with seeds not enclosed in fruit"],["Deciduous","Losing its leaves each year"],["Cycad","An ancient palm-like seed plant"]],
f:["Ginkgo trees are male or female.","Ginkgos survived the Hiroshima bombing.","Ginkgos are native to China."],
fun:"A ginkgo tree’s leaves can all fall in a single day, covering the ground in a golden carpet."});

ADDART({id:"lotus",t:"Lotuses and Water Lilies",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪷",w:"Nelumbo nucifera",r:["aquaticplants","flower","wetlands","lakes","seeds","buddhism","hinduism"],
p:[["Clean leaves","Lotus leaves shed water and dirt, keeping themselves clean."],["Not related","Lotuses and water lilies look alike but are not close relatives."],["Long-lived seeds","Lotus seeds have sprouted after about 1,300 years."]],
l:"Lotuses and water lilies are flowering plants that grow in ponds, lakes and slow rivers, rooted in the mud with leaves and flowers at or above the surface. They look similar, but they are not closely related. Water lilies, of which there are about 70 species, have leaves and flowers that usually float flat on the water. Lotuses, of which there are two species, hold their round leaves and large pink, white or yellow flowers high above the water on long stalks. Surprisingly, DNA studies show that lotuses are more closely related to plane trees and proteas than to water lilies. The sacred lotus of Asia is a powerful symbol of purity in Hinduism and Buddhism, because its flower rises clean and beautiful from muddy water. All parts of the lotus are eaten in Asian cooking: the seeds, the young leaves and the crunchy roots, which have rows of air holes. The giant water lilies of the Amazon have leaves up to about three meters across.",
s:[["The lotus effect","Lotus leaves are covered in microscopic bumps topped with wax. Water forms beads that roll off, picking up dirt as they go. Engineers have copied this ‘lotus effect’ to make self-cleaning paints, glass and fabrics."],["Warm flowers","The sacred lotus can keep its flowers at about 30 to 35 degrees Celsius, even on cool nights, which may attract beetles and other pollinators."],["Seeds that wait","Lotus seeds have very hard coats. In China, seeds found in a dry lakebed were carbon-dated to about 1,300 years old and still sprouted, among the oldest seeds ever grown."],["Giant water lilies","The Amazon water lily’s leaves have upturned rims and ribs underneath that make them strong enough to hold a small child. Its flowers open white on the first evening, trap beetles overnight and reopen pink the next day. In 2022 scientists at Kew Gardens named a new species, Victoria boliviana, with the largest leaves of all."]],
k:[["Water lilies","Family Nymphaeaceae, about 70 species"],["Lotuses","Genus Nelumbo, two species"],["Habitat","Ponds, lakes and slow rivers"],["Largest leaves","Victoria water lilies, about 3 meters across"],["Symbol","Purity in Hinduism and Buddhism"]],
d:[["1849","Giant water lily flowers in England for the first time"],["2022","Victoria boliviana named as a new species"]],
s2:[["Monet’s water lilies","The French painter Claude Monet painted about 250 pictures of the water lilies in his garden pond at Giverny."],["National flowers","The lotus is a national flower of India and Vietnam, and the blue water lily is the national flower of Sri Lanka and Bangladesh."]],
g:[["Aquatic plant","A plant that grows in water"],["Rhizome","An underground stem"],["Lotus effect","The self-cleaning property of lotus leaves"],["Pollinator","An animal that carries pollen between flowers"]],
f:["Lotus leaves are self-cleaning.","Lotuses are not close relatives of water lilies.","Lotus seeds can live for centuries."],
fun:"Lotus seed pods look like showerheads, and the seeds can rattle inside them when dry."});

ADDART({id:"dandelions",t:"Dandelions",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🌼",w:"Taraxacum",r:["flower","seeds","seeddispersal","herbs","invasiveplants","grasses","bee"],
p:[["Lion’s tooth","The name comes from French for ‘lion’s tooth’, after its jagged leaves."],["Seeds that fly","Each seed floats away on a tiny parachute."],["Edible weed","Dandelion leaves, flowers and roots can all be eaten."]],
l:"Dandelions are flowering plants of the genus Taraxacum, best known for their bright yellow flowers and round, fluffy seed heads called clocks. The name comes from the French dent de lion, meaning ‘lion’s tooth’, describing the jagged leaves. Native to Europe and Asia, dandelions have spread to every continent except Antarctica and are among the most familiar weeds of lawns and roadsides. What looks like one flower is actually a head of up to around 200 tiny florets packed together. After flowering, each floret becomes a seed attached to a fluffy parachute called a pappus, and the wind can carry the seeds for kilometers. Dandelions have a long taproot that can regrow the plant if the top is pulled off, which helps make them hard to remove. Many common dandelions produce seeds without pollination, making copies of themselves. All parts of the dandelion are edible: the leaves in salads, the flowers in drinks and the roasted roots as a coffee substitute.",
s:[["A clever parachute","In 2018 scientists showed that air flowing through the bristles of a dandelion pappus forms a stable ring of swirling air, called a separated vortex ring, above the seed. This makes the parachute about four times more efficient than a solid disc of the same size."],["Flowers that close","Dandelion flowers open in the morning and close in the evening and in cloudy weather. The flower stalk droops while the seeds form, then rises again, often taller, to lift the seed head into the wind."],["Food for insects","Dandelions flower early in spring and through much of the year, providing pollen and nectar for bees, hoverflies and butterflies when few other flowers are open."],["Rubber from roots","The Russian dandelion has a milky sap that contains natural rubber. Tire makers have tested it as a source of rubber that could be grown in cooler countries."]],
k:[["Genus","Taraxacum"],["Name meaning","Lion’s tooth"],["Native range","Europe and Asia"],["Seed head","Called a clock"],["Edible parts","Leaves, flowers and roots"]],
d:[["2018","Scientists explain how dandelion seeds fly"]],
s2:[["Blowing wishes","Many children blow on dandelion clocks to make a wish or count the puffs to ‘tell the time’."],["Milky sap","When a dandelion stem is broken, a white, bitter latex oozes out, which helps protect the plant from insects."]],
g:[["Floret","One of the tiny flowers in a flower head"],["Pappus","The fluffy parachute on a seed"],["Taproot","A long main root"],["Weed","A plant growing where people do not want it"]],
f:["A dandelion head is made of many tiny flowers.","Dandelion seeds fly on parachutes.","Dandelions are edible."],
fun:"A dandelion seed can stay airborne long enough to travel more than 100 kilometers on a strong, steady wind, though most land nearby."});

ADDART({id:"peatlands",t:"Bogs and Peatlands",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🟫",w:"Peatland",r:["wetlands","carboncycle","climchange","ferns","carnivplant","fossilfuels","conservation"],
p:[["Carbon stores","Peatlands hold more carbon than all the world’s forests combined."],["Slow decay","Waterlogged ground stops dead plants from rotting fully."],["Bog bodies","Peat bogs have preserved people who died thousands of years ago."]],
l:"Peatlands are wetlands where the ground is made of peat, a thick, spongy layer of partly decayed plants. They form where the soil stays so wet that dead plants cannot fully rot, because the water keeps out the oxygen that decomposers need. Over thousands of years peat builds up, usually by about a millimeter a year, and it can be many meters deep. There are two main kinds: bogs, which get their water only from rain and are acidic and low in nutrients, and fens, which are fed by groundwater and streams. Peatlands cover only about 3 percent of Earth’s land, from the cold north of Canada, Scandinavia and Russia to the tropical swamp forests of Southeast Asia, the Congo Basin and the Amazon. Yet they store about a third of all the carbon held in soils, more than all the world’s forests combined. When peatlands are drained for farming or forestry, or the peat is dug up as fuel or garden compost, they dry out and release carbon dioxide, and drained peat can burn for months.",
s:[["Sphagnum moss","Many bogs are built by sphagnum moss, which can hold up to about 20 times its dry weight in water and makes its surroundings acidic. These conditions slow decay and favor unusual plants, such as sundews and pitcher plants, which catch insects to get nutrients."],["Bog bodies","Hundreds of human bodies have been found in the bogs of northern Europe, preserved for up to thousands of years by the cold, acidic and oxygen-poor water. Tollund Man, found in Denmark in 1950, died about 2,400 years ago and is so well preserved that the stubble on his chin can be seen."],["Peat fuel","In places with few trees, such as Ireland and Scotland, people cut and dried peat for fuel for centuries. Large-scale peat burning in power stations has largely ended in Ireland, and many countries are phasing out peat compost for gardens."],["Restoring peatlands","Blocking drainage ditches to rewet peatlands can stop carbon losses and bring back wildlife. Tropical peat fires, especially in Indonesia in dry years such as 2015, have caused huge carbon emissions and choking haze across Southeast Asia."]],
k:[["Share of land","About 3 percent"],["Share of soil carbon","About one third"],["Peat growth","About 1 millimeter a year"],["Main kinds","Bogs and fens"],["Builder plant","Sphagnum moss"]],
d:[["1950","Tollund Man found in a Danish bog"],["1984","Lindow Man found in England"],["2015","Peat fires in Indonesia cause severe haze"],["2017","Huge peatland mapped in the Congo Basin"]],
s2:[["Congo peatlands","In 2017 scientists announced that the Cuvette Centrale in the Congo Basin holds the largest tropical peatland, storing around 30 billion tonnes of carbon."],["Bog butter","Lumps of butter buried in Irish bogs, some thousands of years old, are still found by peat cutters."]],
g:[["Peat","Partly decayed plant matter that builds up in wetlands"],["Bog","A rain-fed, acidic peatland"],["Fen","A peatland fed by groundwater"],["Sphagnum","A moss that forms bogs"]],
f:["Peatlands store more carbon than forests.","Bogs are fed by rain.","Bodies can be preserved in peat bogs."],
fun:"Peat fires can smolder underground through winter and flare up again the next year, earning the name ‘zombie fires’."});

ADDART({id:"mimosa",t:"The Sensitive Plant",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🌿",w:"Mimosa pudica",r:["plantsenses","plantdefenses","leaves","carnivplant","invasiveplants","flower","cell"],
p:[["Touch-me-not","Its leaves fold up within seconds when touched."],["Water power","The movement comes from cells quickly losing water."],["Plant memory","It can learn to ignore harmless repeated touches."]],
l:"The sensitive plant, Mimosa pudica, is a small, creeping plant famous for folding its leaves when touched. Its feathery leaves are made of many small leaflets, and a touch, a shake or heat makes them fold together in pairs within a few seconds, while the whole leaf stalk droops. After several minutes the leaves slowly open again. The plant also folds its leaves at night. Native to Central and South America, it now grows throughout the tropics, where it can be a weed of fields and lawns, and it is sold as a houseplant in cooler countries. It has small, fluffy pink or purple flower heads shaped like pom-poms. Its names reflect its behavior: pudica is Latin for ‘shy’ or ‘bashful’, and in many languages it is called touch-me-not or sleeping grass. Scientists think the folding may protect the plant by startling plant-eating animals, making it look less tasty or shaking insects off.",
s:[["How it moves","At the base of each leaflet and leaf stalk is a joint-like swelling called a pulvinus. When the plant is touched, an electrical signal travels through the leaf, and cells in the pulvinus quickly lose water and shrink, so the leaf folds. Later the cells take up water again and the leaf reopens."],["Learning plants","In a 2014 experiment, sensitive plants were dropped a short distance again and again. After a while they stopped folding their leaves, as if they had learned the drops were harmless, and they still ignored the drops weeks later, yet they still folded when shaken in a new way."],["A sleeping plant","In the 1700s the French scientist Jean-Jacques d’Ortous de Mairan put a sensitive plant in a dark cupboard and found that its leaves still opened and closed on a daily rhythm. It was one of the first experiments to show that living things have internal body clocks."],["Other moving plants","Venus flytraps snap shut in a fraction of a second, and the telegraph plant moves small leaflets in jerky circles. Many plants, such as beans, fold their leaves at night, a habit called sleep movements."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Mimosa pudica"],["Native range","Central and South America"],["Movement","Leaves fold when touched"],["Time to fold","A few seconds"],["Time to reopen","Several minutes"],["Family","Fabaceae (legumes)"]],
d:[["1729","De Mairan shows the plant keeps a daily rhythm in the dark"],["2014","Experiment suggests the plant can ‘learn’"]],
s2:[["A legume","The sensitive plant belongs to the pea and bean family. Like many legumes, its roots host bacteria that capture nitrogen from the air."],["Prickly stems","Its stems carry small prickles, another defense against hungry animals."]],
g:[["Pulvinus","A swollen joint at the base of a leaf that moves it"],["Leaflet","One of the small parts of a compound leaf"],["Circadian rhythm","A daily internal body clock"],["Legume","A plant of the pea and bean family"]],
f:["The sensitive plant folds its leaves when touched.","It moves by changing water pressure in cells.","It folds its leaves at night."],
fun:"Folding its leaves takes energy, which may be why the plant ‘learned’ to stop folding at harmless drops."});

ADDART({id:"seeddispersal",t:"How Seeds Travel",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🌰",w:"Seed dispersal",r:["seeds","fruits","dandelions","coconut","maples","birds","ants"],
p:[["Moving on","Seeds travel away from their parents to find space and light."],["Wind, water and animals","Plants use many ways to spread seeds."],["Exploding pods","Some plants fling their seeds away."]],
l:"Seed dispersal is the way plants spread their seeds away from the parent plant. Plants cannot walk, but seeds that fall right beneath their parent must compete with it for light, water and nutrients and are more easily found by seed-eating animals and diseases. By traveling, seeds can reach new places to grow, including islands and open ground after a fire or flood. Plants have evolved many methods. Some seeds are carried by the wind on parachutes, like dandelions, or on wings, like maple and ash seeds. Others float on water, like coconuts, which can drift across oceans. Many plants wrap their seeds in sweet, colorful fruit that animals eat, then drop the seeds in their droppings, often far away. Some seeds have hooks that catch on fur and clothing, and some plants have pods that dry and burst, flinging seeds away. Ants carry some seeds to their nests, and people spread seeds of crops and weeds around the world.",
s:[["Wind","Wind-dispersed seeds are usually small and light. Maple seeds, called samaras, spin like helicopter blades as they fall, which slows them so the wind can carry them farther. Orchid seeds are as fine as dust and can blow for great distances."],["Water","Coconuts have a thick, fibrous, air-filled husk that lets them float for months in seawater and still sprout. Plants of riverbanks and mangroves often have floating seeds or seedlings."],["Animals","Birds, bats, monkeys, elephants and fish all spread seeds. Some seeds sprout better after passing through an animal’s gut, which softens their coats. Squirrels and jays bury nuts and acorns to eat later and forget some of them, which then grow into trees. Burdock’s hooked burs inspired the Swiss engineer George de Mestral to invent Velcro in the 1940s."],["Explosions and ants","As the pods of plants such as touch-me-nots and squirting cucumbers dry or ripen, pressure builds until they burst and fling seeds. The sandbox tree of the Americas can hurl its seeds at about 250 kilometers per hour. Many plants attach a fatty snack, called an elaiosome, to their seeds, so ants carry them home, eat the snack and leave the seed to sprout in the rich soil of the nest."]],
k:[["Main methods","Wind, water, animals, bursting and ants"],["Wind example","Dandelions and maples"],["Water example","Coconuts"],["Animal example","Fruits, nuts and burs"],["Invention inspired","Velcro"]],
d:[["1941","George de Mestral notices burs on his dog, leading to Velcro"]],
s2:[["Fire seeds","Some pines and other plants keep their seeds in sealed cones that only open after a fire, releasing seeds onto freshly cleared, ash-rich ground."],["Long-distance travelers","Seeds reaching remote islands such as Hawaii arrived by sea, by wind or stuck to and inside birds."]],
g:[["Dispersal","Spreading out"],["Samara","A winged seed, such as a maple’s"],["Bur","A seed case with hooks"],["Elaiosome","A food body on a seed that attracts ants"]],
f:["Coconuts can float across oceans.","Burs inspired Velcro.","Some seeds are spread by ants."],
fun:"The squirting cucumber can shoot its seeds several meters in a jet of slimy juice."});

ADDART({id:"plantdefenses",t:"How Plants Defend Themselves",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🌵",w:"Plant defense against herbivory",r:["plantsenses","poisonousplants","cactus","ants","mimosa","medicinalplants","symbiosis"],
p:[["Thorns and spines","Sharp parts keep hungry animals away."],["Chemical weapons","Many plants make poisons and bad-tasting chemicals."],["Calling for help","Some plants signal to insects that attack their attackers."]],
l:"Plants cannot run away from the animals that eat them, so they have evolved many ways to defend themselves. Physical defenses include thorns, spines and prickles, tough leaves, hard seed coats and sticky or stinging hairs. A stinging nettle’s hairs are like tiny glass needles that break and inject irritating chemicals. Chemical defenses are even more widespread. Plants make thousands of substances that taste bitter, upset digestion or are poisonous, such as nicotine in tobacco, caffeine in coffee and tea, and cyanide-releasing compounds in cassava and apple seeds. Many medicines, spices and drugs come from these defensive chemicals. Some plants make more of them only when they are attacked, and some release smells that attract predators of the insects eating them. Other plants recruit bodyguards: acacia trees in Central America give ants shelter and food in exchange for protection. Animals in turn evolve ways around these defenses, in an evolutionary arms race.",
s:[["Thorns, spines and prickles","Botanists use these words for different things. Thorns are sharp branches, as on hawthorns. Spines are modified leaves, as on cacti. Prickles grow from the outer skin, as on roses, which is why rose ‘thorns’ snap off easily."],["Poisons","Foxglove contains chemicals that affect the heart, from which the medicine digoxin was developed. Milkweed sap is toxic, and monarch caterpillars store its poisons, making the butterflies poisonous to birds. Chili peppers make capsaicin, which deters mammals but not birds, which spread the seeds."],["Calling for help","When caterpillars chew corn, cotton or bean leaves, the plants release scents that attract parasitic wasps, which lay their eggs in the caterpillars. Tomato plants attacked by insects make chemicals that make leaves harder to digest, and nearby plants may respond too."],["Ant bodyguards","Bullhorn acacias have hollow thorns where ants live and produce nectar and protein-rich tips that feed them. The ants sting animals that touch the tree and cut away vines that climb on it. Trees whose ants are removed are quickly damaged."]],
k:[["Physical defenses","Thorns, spines, prickles, hairs, tough leaves"],["Chemical defenses","Poisons, bitter tastes, sticky resins"],["Example poison","Nicotine, cyanide compounds"],["Bodyguards","Ants on acacia trees"],["Result","An evolutionary arms race"]],
s2:[["Silica","Grasses contain silica, the material of sand, which wears down the teeth of grazing animals. Grazers have evolved teeth that keep growing to cope."],["Giraffes and acacias","African acacias have long thorns, but giraffes have tough, flexible tongues and lips to strip leaves from between them."]],
g:[["Herbivore","An animal that eats plants"],["Spine","A sharp, modified leaf"],["Prickle","A sharp outgrowth of the outer skin"],["Arms race","A contest in which each side keeps evolving to outdo the other"]],
f:["Cactus spines are modified leaves.","Rose ‘thorns’ are really prickles.","Some plants recruit ants as bodyguards."],
fun:"Caffeine in nectar can give bees a buzz that helps them remember a flower and come back."});

ADDART({id:"willows",t:"Willows",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🌳",w:"Willow",r:["trees","medicinalplants","rivers","wetlands","roots","oaks","basketry"],
p:[["Waterside trees","Willows grow along rivers, lakes and wetlands."],["Nature’s aspirin","Willow bark led to the discovery of aspirin."],["Easy to grow","A cut willow branch pushed into wet ground can grow roots."]],
l:"Willows are trees and shrubs of the genus Salix, with about 350 to 400 species growing mostly in the cooler and temperate parts of the Northern Hemisphere. They range from tall trees such as the weeping willow, whose long branches hang down to the ground, to tiny arctic willows only a few centimeters high that creep over the tundra. Willows love water and are common along rivers, lakes and wetlands, where their dense roots help hold soil in place. Their flowers grow in fuzzy spikes called catkins, which give pussy willows their name, and male and female flowers usually grow on separate plants. Willow bark has been used to ease pain and fever since ancient times, and in the 1800s chemists isolated salicin from it. This led to the development of aspirin, one of the most widely used medicines in the world. Willow wood is light and flexible and is used for baskets, furniture and cricket bats.",
s:[["From bark to aspirin","Ancient texts from Sumer and Egypt mention willow for pain, and the Greek physician Hippocrates recommended it. In 1828 the German chemist Johann Buchner isolated salicin from willow bark. Chemists later made salicylic acid and, in 1897, Felix Hoffmann at the Bayer company made a gentler form, acetylsalicylic acid, sold as aspirin from 1899."],["Growing from cuttings","Willows root very easily. Their stems contain natural rooting chemicals, and gardeners soak willow twigs in water to make ‘willow water’ that helps other cuttings root. Willow branches are planted to stop riverbanks from washing away."],["Baskets and bats","Young willow shoots, called withies or osiers, are cut and woven into baskets, fences and sculptures. Cricket bats are traditionally made from the light, tough wood of a variety of white willow."],["Food for wildlife","Willow catkins are among the first sources of pollen and nectar for bees in spring. Many caterpillars feed on willow leaves, and beavers eat willow bark and use the branches to build dams."]],
k:[["Genus","Salix"],["Species","About 350–400"],["Habitat","Riversides, wetlands and tundra"],["Flowers","Catkins"],["Medicine","Source of salicin, which led to aspirin"],["Uses","Baskets, cricket bats, riverbank protection"]],
d:[["1828","Salicin isolated from willow bark"],["1897","Felix Hoffmann makes acetylsalicylic acid"],["1899","Aspirin goes on sale"]],
s2:[["Weeping willows","The weeping willow came originally from China and was carried along trade routes to Europe and beyond. Its drooping shape has made it a symbol of sadness and remembrance in art and poetry."],["Tiny tree","The dwarf willow of the Arctic and high mountains may be one of the smallest trees in the world, often only 1 to 6 centimeters tall."]],
g:[["Catkin","A long, fuzzy cluster of tiny flowers"],["Salicin","A pain-relieving chemical from willow bark"],["Cutting","A piece of a plant used to grow a new one"],["Osier","A willow shoot used for weaving"]],
f:["Aspirin was developed from willow bark.","Willows grow easily from cuttings.","Cricket bats are made of willow."],
fun:"A willow branch used as a fence post can take root and grow into a tree."});
